PART 1 -- E_G, and the reduction that makes it the right statistic here
==============================================================================================================================
  GR: E_G = Omega_m0/f(z) = 0.3134/0.6374 = 0.4917 at z = 0.20
  GR: E_G = Omega_m0/f(z) = 0.3134/0.6921 = 0.4528 at z = 0.32
  framework: beta and Upsilon_gg are LambdaCDM's (item 85's measured beta = 0.447 vs 0.440 requires it),
  so E_G(fw)/E_G(GR) = Upsilon_gm(fw)/Upsilon_gm(LambdaCDM) -- bias-free and, for a matched sample, selection-free.

==============================================================================================================================
PART 2 -- the correlated-mass (two-halo) term from linear theory, validated before use
==============================================================================================================================
  [PASS] 2a linear theory validated before it is used: the transfer function is normalised to sigma_8 = 0.811 by construction, and the correlation function it produces must cross xi = 1 near 5 h^-1 Mpc, which is where LambdaCDM's linear matter correlation length sits   (sigma_8 recovered 0.8110; xi_mm = 1 at r0 = 7.29 Mpc = 4.91 h^-1 Mpc; xi(1 Mpc) = 7.25, xi(5 Mpc) = 1.590, xi(20 Mpc) = 0.2060)
  [PASS] 2b the two-halo Delta-Sigma must be insensitive to where the line-of-sight integral is truncated -- Sigma itself is not, but Delta-Sigma differences it away, and if that failed every number below would be an artefact of the box   (chi_max = 150 Mpc gives 0.2681, chi_max = 60 Mpc gives 0.2680 Msun/pc^2 (0.0% apart) at R = 2 Mpc, b = 1)

