       3.00          0.3758            -0.0003            -0.0004         0.999
       5.00          0.3913            -0.0003            -0.0003         0.999
  [PASS] 84 THE ITEM'S ANSWER: E_G at 1-5 Mpc is predicted to be GR's, to within a few per cent, on BOTH footings.  The framework's only distinctive term at those scales was the lens's own phantom, and its own external-field effect has removed it.  Item 84 therefore carries no information that item 85 did not already carry, and the E_G machinery -- whose whole purpose is to cancel the galaxy bias and the lens selection -- would be cancelling systematics in front of a signal that is not there   (E_G(framework)/E_G(GR) = 1 + -0.0004 at worst over R = 1-5 Mpc with the EFE (canonical -0.0004, alt -0.0006); WITHOUT the EFE the same calculation gives 1 + 2.11, which is the tens-of-per-cent signal the item was written to look for.  GR's own value is Omega_m/f = 0.492 at z = 0.2)
  [PASS] 84f THE ONE BRANCH THAT WOULD HAVE SHOWN UP IS ALREADY DEAD.  If the kernel acted on the quasi-linear correlated field rather than only on bound baryons, every megaparsec-scale lensing amplitude would be multiplied by nu(e_N) and E_G would be several times GR's -- excluded independently by item 85's beta and by the published E_G measurements at 10-50 h^-1 Mpc.  This item's contribution is to put a number on how far that branch sits from the data   (an unprotected kernel at e_N = 0.01 gives nu = 10.51, i.e. E_G = 4.76 against GR's 0.45 and the published Reyes+2010 measurement 0.39 +- 0.06 at 10-50 h^-1 Mpc, z = 0.32 -- about 73 sigma out.  The framework survives only through its linear-growth theorem, which is item 85's conclusion restated)
  [PASS] 84g MUTATION CONTROL 2: with a_0 -> 0 the kernel is off, the phantom must vanish identically, and the lens must reduce to its bare baryons.  It does -- so every number above is the a_0 term and not a projection artefact   (a_0 -> 0 gives Delta-Sigma(2 Mpc) = 0.00327 Msun/pc^2 against the bare point-mass value M_b/(pi R^2) = 0.00327)

==============================================================================================================================
WHAT IS MISSING, STATED PLAINLY
==============================================================================================================================
  The item asked for E_G, which needs projected galaxy clustering w_p(R) for the SAME isolated lens sample as the
  Delta-Sigma above.  That is not in this repository and could not be fetched for a matched sample; the published
  E_G measurements use LRG or BOSS/GAMA lenses at R >= 5 h^-1 Mpc, a different selection at larger scales.  So the
  E_G measurement itself is NOT RUNNABLE here.  What has changed is that it no longer matters: the quantity it would
  measure is predicted to be GR's to a few per cent, so no achievable precision would separate the theories.

==============================================================================================================================
VERDICT
==============================================================================================================================
  Item 84 is WITHDRAWN as a discriminating test, on the framework's own physics rather than for want of data.
  E_G(framework)/E_G(GR) = 1 + -0.000 at 1-5 Mpc on both footings, against 1 + 2.11 if the EFE is left out.
  The reason is the external-field effect:
  solved in QUMOND, an isolated galaxy's phantom saturates in mass at r ~ r_M/sqrt(e_N) = 40-220 kpc for these
  lenses, so beyond a few hundred kpc the lens is a point mass of nu(e) M_b and everything else that is measured is
  correlated mass -- which the framework and LambdaCDM share, by item 85's own load-bearing theorem.

  THE BY-PRODUCT IS THE PART WORTH KEEPING, AND IT IS A CAVEAT ON TWO ITEMS ON THE KEEPER LIST.  The measured 1/r
  lensing law out to 2.6 Mpc (items 1 and 72) cannot be the isolated lens's own MOND phantom beyond ~0.1 Mpc if the
  framework's own external-field effect is applied; on these data a phantom cut off at 0.1 Mpc plus correlated mass
  contributes only 6% of the measured signal at 1.9 Mpc, so the megaparsec end of that law is correlated mass
  in the framework exactly as it is in LambdaCDM.  Those keepers should be read as 'the TOTAL profile is close to
  1/R over 0.05-2.6 Mpc', which is what B21 measured and is a real fact about the data, and not as 'the MOND boost
  itself has been seen to extend to megaparsecs' -- the framework's own field equation does not allow that.

  AND ONE THING THE ITEM DID NOT SETTLE.  My correlated-mass model is linear theory, a lower bound at these radii;
  it under-predicts the measurement by factors of 1.1 to 5.4 on its own.  So the amplitude at 1-3 Mpc cannot be
  used for
  model selection here, and the framework's dark-sector fork -- does the dust virialise? -- is what it would decide.

